--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertThrows` matches the class its body threw, an ancestor included, and `assertDoesNotThrow` is the claim that a body completes -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed` and `rule:testing/runner-is-strict`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two members on § 4's roster whose subject is a `callable` rather than a
// value: each runs the body and judges what came back. What a case can observe
// is which bodies each accepts, what a refusal says, and that both agree with
// the `try`/`catch` a reader would otherwise have written by hand.

use Core\Test;

final class Body {
    // The bodies are named methods so that the member and the hand-written
    // `try` below run the *same* code rather than two spellings of it.
    public static function parse(): void {
        throw new ParseError("the input is not JSON");
    }

    public static function quiet(): void {
    }
}

int $agreed = 0;

// 1. The exact class. `ParseError::class` folds to the fully qualified name as
// a `string` constant, so the expectation is a name and no class value has to
// exist for a member to take one.
// Written out in full here and through the `use` below, which are one member
// either way.
Core\Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, ParseError::class);
Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, ParseError::class);
echo "the exact class holds\n";

// 2. An ancestor matches, which is what a `catch` naming that class would bind:
// `ParseError` extends `RuntimeError` extends `Throwable`.
Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, RuntimeError::class);
Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, Throwable::class);
echo "every ancestor holds\n";

// 3. The wrong class fails, naming the class the body *did* throw and that
// throw's own message -- which together are what say whether the test or the
// code under it is wrong.
try {
    Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, LogicError::class);
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $wrong) {
    echo $wrong->message, "\n";
}

// 4. A body that throws nothing is the other failure, with ADR 0063 R2's
// `{message: ...}` bag in front of the report.
try {
    Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::quiet(); }, ParseError::class, {message: "the decode refuses"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $silent) {
    echo $silent->message, "\n";
}

// 5. § 20's member is the same three edges judged the other way round, and the
// assertion a test whose whole claim is "this completes" writes.
Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::quiet(); });
Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::quiet(); });
echo "a body that completes holds\n";
try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, {message: "the config loads"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $threw) {
    echo $threw->message, "\n";
}

// 6. The verdict that propagates is the assertion's own `Core\Test\Failure`
// and never the exception it is reporting -- so a `catch` around either member
// sees one class whichever way the body went.
try {
    Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, LogicError::class);
} catch (ParseError $leaked) {
    echo "the reported throw leaked\n";
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $verdict) {
    echo "the verdict is the assertion's own\n";
}

// 7. Agreement: each member answers what the hand-written `try`/`catch` of the
// same question answers. Counted rather than read off a line, so a member that
// grew a second reading fails here while still looking right above.
bool $held = false;
bool $caught = false;

try {
    Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, ParseError::class);
    $held = true;
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $one) {
}
try {
    Body::parse();
} catch (ParseError $two) {
    $caught = true;
}
if ($held == $caught) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }

$held = false;
$caught = false;
try {
    Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); }, LogicError::class);
    $held = true;
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $three) {
}
try {
    Body::parse();
} catch (LogicError $four) {
    $caught = true;
} catch (Throwable $five) {
}
if ($held == $caught) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }

$held = false;
$caught = false;
try {
    Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Body::quiet(); }, ParseError::class);
    $held = true;
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $six) {
}
try {
    Body::quiet();
} catch (ParseError $seven) {
    $caught = true;
}
if ($held == $caught) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }

$held = false;
$caught = false;
try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::quiet(); });
    $held = true;
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $eight) {
}
try {
    Body::quiet();
} catch (Throwable $nine) {
    $caught = true;
}
if ($held != $caught) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }

$held = false;
$caught = false;
try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::parse(); });
    $held = true;
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $ten) {
}
try {
    Body::parse();
} catch (Throwable $eleven) {
    $caught = true;
}
if ($held != $caught) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }

echo "agreed=", $agreed, "/5\n";
--EXPECT--
the exact class holds
every ancestor holds
Core\Test::assertThrows failed: `$body` threw ParseError, not LogicError: the input is not JSON
the decode refuses: Core\Test::assertThrows failed: `$body` returned without throwing ParseError
a body that completes holds
the config loads: Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow failed: `$body` threw: the input is not JSON
the verdict is the assertion's own
agreed=5/5
